WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.100
First reactions to The Rise of Skywalker were
mostly positive but clearly mixed.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.300
Now, it's time to dive into the full-length
reviews to see how critics truly feel about

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.970
what director and co-writer J.J. Abrams did
with the final installment of the Skywalker

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:15.980
saga.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.940
Warning: mild spoilers ahead!

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:23.630
Of the reviews on Rotten Tomatoes that register
as "Rotten", that is, ones accompanied by

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:28.420
ratings below 60 percent a large portion of
them take issue with the general feeling of

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.610
forced wish-granting woven throughout the
film.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:36.290
This is to say that many critics were displeased
by the fact that The Rise of Skywalker feels

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:41.540
like it's pandering to the audience including
people who were angry with the events of writer-director

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.390
Rian Johnson's The Last Jedi, a Star Wars
installment that divided fans and caused them

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.810
to bicker with critics who praised the film
for the past two years.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:53.450
"The beginning of their end.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:55.320
Destroy it!"

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.730
Michael O'Sullivan of The Washington Post
was one of a number of critics conflicted

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.610
over the fan service crammed into The Rise
of Skywalker, arguing that the film "panders

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.070
wildly" and concludes the Skywalker saga,
quote, "with a story that delivers to the

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:14.170
faithful exactly the movie they wanted", which
may or may not be a good thing.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.830
He wrote in his review,

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.159
"Everybody wants a happy ending.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:21.860
But that doesn't mean that we should always
get the one we want.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.659
It's fine, if also cliche, to be reminded
that good will triumph over evil.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:31.640
But it would make for a deeper and more powerful
lesson one that, after nine movies, might

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:37.689
leave a lasting dent in the heart if the hero
actually had to give up something, or someone,

00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:40.890
that didn't feel like a tiniest bit of a cop-out."

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:45.810
The Film Stage critic Jordan Raup wrote that
The Rise of Skywalker is the antithesis to

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:51.340
The Last Jedi, in that it doesn't take many
risks and undoes a lot of what preceded it.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:52.340
He wrote,

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.790
"A direct contradiction to Johnson's repeated
mantra of letting old things die, this ninth

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:03.049
and final film in the series is wholly about
the desire to uncover the past to unlock one's

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:08.060
true identity and honor the generations and
iconic characters that came before.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.980
By lacking a sense of vision in embracing
what came before and ignoring the recently

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.450
laid path for where this story could go, The
Rise of Skywalker is not only a disappointing

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:24.360
end to this saga, it's also an ill-fated harbinger
for Disney's future in storytelling."

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.860
Other critics agreed with Raup's point that
The Rise of Skywalker plays it safe and doesn't

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.340
take the risks that it could have, decisions
that many feel are to the film's detriment.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:39.090
USA Today's Brian Truitt, who gave The Rise
of Skywalker two-and-a-half stars out of four,

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.470
wrote in his review,

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:45.390
"It's impressively ambitious, though great
new personalities and fresh storytelling suffer

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:50.140
for the sake of fan service...Some of the
liberties Rian Johnson's Last Jedi took with

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:55.569
the nostalgic Star Wars status quo are rethought
here, so get ready for a disturbance in the

00:02:55.569 --> 00:02:56.569
fandom."

00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:02.350
Over at Slate, Sam Adams wrote that Abrams
attempted to "make a movie no one would hate"

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:06.870
instead of one that some might love, resulting
in a film that's only satisfying because it's

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:08.629
been "force-fed" to viewers.

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:09.629
He added,

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:15.300
"The haste with which The Rise of Skywalker
rushes to undo its predecessor is almost comical

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:21.220
at first, at least before its capitulation
to the franchise's most toxic fans turns outright

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:22.739
contemptible."

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:27.291
In other less-than-great reviews of The Rise
of Skywalker, a common gripe was that the

00:03:27.291 --> 00:03:30.370
film comes across confused and messy.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.459
Dan Murrell of Screen Junkies said in his
video review,

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:38.519
"There seems to have been absolutely no road
map as to what these movies were going to

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:40.480
be, and it shows."

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.680
Meanwhile, Uproxx senior entertainment reporter
Mike Ryan argued that the film is perplexing,

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.760
clumsy, and totally overstuffed, writing,

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:55.020
"J.J. Abrams' Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker
is the most convoluted of all the Star Wars

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.020
movies.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:59.959
It feels like three full movies worth of plot
crammed into one film.

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:04.459
The stories in the other Star Wars movies,
even the Prequels, have a way of bringing

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:06.769
a viewer into that world.

00:04:06.769 --> 00:04:10.040
Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker never lets
us in.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:16.880
It, instead, keeps us at arms length so it
can use almost its entire first half as exposition.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.860
Just character after character explaining
things."

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:24.580
Ryan also felt that The Rise of Skywalker
panders to a certain type of Star Wars fan,

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:25.580
adding,

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:29.720
"There were times it felt like The Rise of
Skywalker was put together by reading angry

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:34.410
Reddit boards, just throwing in anything a
fan might possibly want to see.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.840
It really is baffling."

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.320
Over at Vanity Fair, Richard Lawson expanded
on the point Ryan brought up, writing in his

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:42.320
review,

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.470
"There doesn't seem to be any real organic
idea animating Rise of Skywalker; instead

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:53.300
it feels cobbled together from notes stuffed
in the suggestion boxes of Reddit and Twitter...I

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:58.520
don't think Rise of Skywalker is ill-intentioned,
exactly...but it takes no pleasure in its

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.220
own existence."

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.250
Where there are detractors, there are also
cheerleaders, and The Rise of Skywalker has

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:09.600
plenty of enthusiastic critics rallying behind
it, ones who had no trouble giving the film

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.280
flawless five-out-of-five-star reviews and
praising it as a satisfying conclusion to

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.690
the four-decade-long Star Wars story.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:21.620
Jordan Hoffman at The Guardian was one such
critic who stamped The Rise of Skywalker with

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.360
a perfect rating, writing,

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.840
"The movie snaps together like a jigsaw puzzle,
a series of concluding beats that seem inevitable

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:33.570
and perfect, and designed to please all parties,
so long as you don't dwell on the logic too

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:34.660
much."

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:39.350
Newsday's Rafer Guzmán gave the film three
out of four stars, and said that the main

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:44.889
takeaway is that, quote, "after more than
40 years, the Star Wars series comes in for

00:05:44.889 --> 00:05:46.440
a smooth landing."

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.790
Richard Roeper at The Chicago-Sun Times had
even more kind things to say about The Rise

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:56.389
of Skywalker, though he ultimately felt the
film was really good but not great, writing,

00:05:56.389 --> 00:06:03.100
"The Rise of Skywalker is an action-filled,
plot-packed, unabashedly sentimental, cameo-heavy,

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.389
rousing adventure.

00:06:04.389 --> 00:06:08.169
The special effects are as fantastic as you'd
expect them to be.

00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:13.320
The performances are earnest and, in some
cases, emotionally impactful...The Rise of

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:19.310
Skywalker rarely comes close to touching greatness,
but it's a solid, visually dazzling and warmhearted

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.370
victory for the Force of quality filmmaking."

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:27.720
Someone who did feel The Rise of Skywalker
is capital-F fantastic was The Seattle Times'

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.130
Soren Andersen, who wrote in part,

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:36.310
"Epic it indeed is, full of magnificent set
pieces sprawling space battles and incandescent

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:41.880
lightsaber duels gripping performances and
a number of truly stunning surprises."

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.810
Digital Spy's Ian Sandwell was noticeably
impressed by The Rise of Skywalker, calling

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:51.930
it an "emotional, thrilling, and satisfying
end to the Skywalker Saga, with plenty of

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.419
surprises up its sleeve."

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:54.419
He wrote,

00:06:54.419 --> 00:07:00.270
"The Rise of Skywalker shines with emotional
beats that fans won't forget, as well as crowd-pleasing

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:05.330
payoffs that have been decades in the making...Given
the task that Abrams faced when he returned

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:09.630
to the franchise, Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker
is an impressive achievement.

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:13.819
It won't please everybody, of course, but
there will be plenty who find it ends the

00:07:13.819 --> 00:07:16.099
Skywalker Saga on a high."

00:07:16.099 --> 00:07:20.680
So it sounds like some people are going to
love The Rise of Skywalker, and some people

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:24.840
are going to hate The Rise of Skywalker, which
is no big surprise.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.610
If you find yourself in the latter camp, take
heart: A new episode of The Mandalorian airs

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:37.360
December 27th!

